    ],
    "view": {
      "mode": "single",
      "stem": "Write each expression as a binomial times a trinomial:\n\nI. $(a+6)^3-1$\n\nII. $(y-2)^3+27$\n\nIII. $(x+3)^3-64$\n\nIV. $x^3+(x-y)^3$\n\nWhich list gives all four products?",
      "figure_ids": [],
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "I: $(a+5)(a^2+13a+43)$\n\nII: $(y+1)(y^2-7y+19)$\n\nIII: $(x-1)(x^2+10x+37)$\n\nIV: $(2x-y)(x^2-xy+y^2)$",
          "figure_ids": []
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "I: $(a+5)(a^2+13a+43)$\n\nII: $(y+1)(y^2-7y+19)$\n\nIII: $(x+7)(x^2+10x+37)$\n\nIV: $(2x-y)(x^2-xy+y^2)$",
          "figure_ids": []
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "I: $(a+5)(a^2+13a+43)$\n\nII: $(y+1)(y^2-3y+19)$\n\nIII: $(x-1)(x^2+4x+37)$\n\nIV: $(2x-y)(x^2-xy+y^2)$",
          "figure_ids": []
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "I: $(a+5)(a^2+13a+43)$\n\nII: $(y+1)(y^2-7y+19)$\n\nIII: $(x-1)(x^2+10x+37)$\n\nIV: $(2x-y)(x^2+xy+y^2)$",
          "figure_ids": []
        }
      ]
    },
    "intent": {
      "task": "Factor all four composed cube expressions into the explicitly requested binomial/trinomial shape.",
      "source_solution": "Cube bases (a+6,1),(y-2,3),(x+3,4),(x,x-y) give first factors a+5,y+1,x-1,2x-y. Expanding their quadratic factors gives a^2+13a+43,y^2-7y+19,x^2+10x+37,x^2-xy+y^2. For III specifically, ((x+3)-4)((x+3)^2+4(x+3)+16)=(x-1)(x^2+6x+9+4x+12+16)=(x-1)(x^2+10x+37).",
      "unit_reason": "The complete list tests treating an entire expression as a cube base and then simplifying its trinomial, rather than separate substitution clicks. Row III now has competing first-factor and square-cross-term results to judge. B has the same correct I, II and IV rows as A and differs only in III, so those other rows cannot eliminate it. Whole-list selection still does not certify independently produced working or separate mastery of every subpart.",
      "concept_blocks": [
        7,
        10
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Supplied-factor recognition does not establish independent expansion and factor production."
      ],
      "figure_observations": []
    },
    "key": {
      "correct": [
        "A"
      ],
      "rationales": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "truth": "correct",
          "derivation": "Apply the appropriate cube identity before expanding each composed base; all four required shapes are obtained.",
          "misconception": null
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "truth": "incorrect",
          "derivation": "I, II and IV now match the correct complete option. Only III is false: its first factor must be (x+3)-4=x-1, not x+7. At x=0 the candidate gives 259 instead of the original -37.",
          "misconception": "For III, use (x+3)+4=x+7 as the first factor while leaving (x+3)^2+4(x+3)+16=x^2+10x+37 unchanged."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "truth": "incorrect",
          "derivation": "Row II needs (y-2)^2-3(y-2)+9=y^2-7y+19. Row III needs (x+3)^2+4(x+3)+16=x^2+10x+37; omitting +6x gives x^2+4x+37. At x=2 the candidate product is 49 instead of the original 61.",
          "misconception": "Expand (y-2)^2 as y^2+4, omitting -4y, then retain -3y+6+9 to get y^2-3y+19. For III, apply the same missing-cross-term procedure: (x+3)^2 is falsely x^2+9, and adding 4x+12+16 yields x^2+4x+37 with first factor x-1 unchanged."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "truth": "incorrect",
          "derivation": "Row IV's quadratic is x^2-x(x-y)+(x-y)^2=x^2-xy+y^2.",
          "misconception": "Distribute -x(x-y) as -x^2-xy; then combine that mistaken term with a second mistaken expansion (x-y)^2=x^2+2xy+y^2, yielding x^2+xy+y^2."
        }
      ]
    }
  },
